// Watchdog -- notice when a signal stops arriving.
//
// Silence is the one fault that never announces itself: a sensor that has
// stopped answering looks exactly like a sensor reading a steady value, and a
// CAN node that has fallen off the bus leaves its last frame sitting in the
// buffer forever. The watchdog turns that absence into a signal you can act on.
//
//   #Watchdog wt  Deadline=2000
//   wt.In = Temp                    // any change counts as a sign of life
//   Heater = 0 ? wt.Lost            // fail safe while we are blind
//
// The clock runs from boot, so a source that never says anything at all is
// caught the same way as one that goes quiet later -- there is no grace period
// hiding a device that was dead from the start.
//
// IF YOUR EVENT CARRIES NO VALUE, count it. A frame that arrives with an
// unchanged payload is still proof of life, but `In` would not move and the
// watchdog would trip on a working link:
//
//   wt.In = wt.In + 1 ? F.rx
//
// The counter wraps eventually and that is fine -- only the change is read,
// never the value.
//
// WHY A TICK AND A COUNTER rather than one timer held restarted: a running
// timer cannot be restarted. `T = 1` only arms a timer that has stopped, so
// the familiar "keep kicking it" idiom silently does nothing here. Tk free-runs
// instead and Age accumulates milliseconds, which costs one variable and works.
// Tk.period is therefore the resolution: Lost can be up to one tick late.
//
#module Watchdog
#timer    Tk 100 = 1                  // resolution in ms -- also the tick
#variable In              in  = 0     // the signal being watched
#variable Lost:1 unsigned out = 0     // 1 = nothing heard within the deadline
#variable Deadline            = 1000  // ms of silence that counts as a fault
#variable Age                 = 0     // ms since the last sign of life
#variable Prev                = 0     // the value we last saw

Tk = 1 ? timeout(Tk)

// Age stops at the deadline instead of counting forever: nothing above it means
// anything different, and a device left dead for a month would otherwise walk
// its way to integer overflow and report itself healthy again.
Age = Age + Tk.period ? timeout(Tk)
Age = Deadline ? timeout(Tk) && Age >= Deadline

// A sign of life resets the clock. This rule comes last so it wins the cycle
// where a signal returns exactly as the deadline runs out.
Age = 0, Prev = In ? In != Prev

// `In == Prev` is "nothing arrived this cycle". Without it, a signal returning
// after the deadline had already passed would raise Lost for one cycle on the
// very cycle it came back, because Age is not reset until the commit.
Lost = 1 ? Age >= Deadline && In == Prev
Lost = 0 ? Age <  Deadline
#end
